While India's opposition is protesting against the Chief Election Commissioner (CEC), so are other activists and student groups.&#160;
One such group is pressure group the&#160;Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) which has already led a protest demanding CEC Gyanesh Kumar's resignation in western city of Mumbai, earlier this week. They are scheduled to participate in a similar protest in Bengaluru today and lead one in Delhi on October 10.
CJP co-convener Ashutosh Ranka on Thursday claimed that thousands of the group's volunteers had been detained across states like Maharashtra, Uttar Pradesh and West Bengal "on the grounds that they were planning to attend the October 10th protest."&#160;
"How many will you arrest? There are over 250 million cockroaches," he said on X, using the group's chosen term for its supporters.
"Modi thinks he can silence dissent by putting people behind bars. The more people you detain, the more people will show up!" CJP Founder Abhijeet Dipke said in a post on X.
Police authorities in these states have not commented on the matter so far.
Skip next section All you need to know about the protests 10/08/2026 October 8, 2026 All you need to know about the protests
Pressure on Kumar intensified in September after the Indian Express &#160;newspaper reported that two of the three election commissioners had formally raised concerns over aspects of the Special Intensive Revisions (SIR)&#160;at least 14 times over the past 10 months.&#160;
The objections relate to new voter registrations, deletion and restoration of voters in the rolls, filing of appeals against orders restoring voters in the SIR process and the custody and security of the entire electoral roll itself, the report said.
In one of the objections, Commissioner Sukhbir Singh Sandhu reportedly&#160;complained that the Kumar&#160;acted against the written advice of himself and Commissioner Vivek Joshi&#160;and claimed his&#160;actions was "unauthorized and illegal."
However, the Election Commission of India (ECI)&#160;issued a statement dismissing&#160;concerns of an internal rift, saying differing views were a "normal" part of deliberations and that all its&#160;actions were lawful.
According to Election Commission data cited by Indian media, over 130 million names have been excluded from the draft electoral rolls since the revision began in October 25. That's roughly the population of Mexico. In India, it adds up to about 15% of the electorate.&#160;
Not all the exclusions are contested as they also took double entries and deaths into account.&#160;
A clearer idea of wrongfully excluded voters can only emerge after the exercise is complete across the nation.&#160;
After denying a rift, the&#160;ECI has since taken a number of measures to improve internal procedures and transparency. It has also directed a "special drive" in 20 states and union territories to include voters who have been left out of the rolls due to solvable errors.&#160;
How do these protests relate to Modi and his party?
The protests against CEC Gyanesh Kumar are partially being viewed as renewed pressure on Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi's government.
The reason behind it may not be immediately obvious since the ECI is meant to be an independent body. But this might help:&#160;
The commission is&#160;responsible for overseeing elections and related projects such as the SIR exercise, which is meant to&#160;remove redundancies in India's electoral rolls.&#160;
The controvers around the Election Commission of India is adding to the political pressure on Narendra Modi's government Image: Ajit Solanki/AP Photo/picture alliance
Opposition leaders and critics have argued that those struck off in the exercise include a heavy proportion of minority voters and that Prime Minister Narendra Modi's BJP is using it as a tool to secure future electoral wins. They have also accused ECI chief Kumar of doing the central government's bidding even though the institute is meant to maintain its independence.
The BJP has dismissed those claims, arguing that those removed largely involve dead voters and illegal infiltrators, thereby protecting India's democracy.
Skip next section India's opposition alliance plans third day of protest against election chief 10/08/2026 October 8, 2026 India's opposition alliance plans third day of protest against election chief
A bloc of Indian political parties, in alliance against Prime Minister Narendra Modi's BJP party, is&#160;expected to stage a protest in central Delhi on Thursday to demand the resignation of India's Chief Election Commissioner (CEC) Gyanesh Kumar over alleged misconduct.&#160;
Opposition leaders including Indian National Congress' Rahul Gandhi and Priyanka Vadra are visibly at the forefront of the protest.
On Tuesday, Gandhi, Vadra, their supporters and over 200 allied Members of Parliament (MPs) marched on the Election Commission of India (ECI) to demand a meeting with Kumar. When blocked by the Delhi police, they began an impromptu sit in which ended with temporary detentions.&#160;
On Wednesday, the INC hosted a lunch-meeting in Delhi for about 200 MPs to discuss action against the CEC. Post lunch, they loaded up in buses to move towards the designated protest site of Jantar Mantar in central Delhi. Eventually on foot, the lawmakers were stopped by the police again.&#160;
A live feed on the YouTube channel of Gandhi's party showed police officials taking him into a vehicle and driving away. His sibling and lawmaker Vadra was also detained but taken to a different police station, as per Indian media.&#160;
Rahul and Priyanka are siblings, born into one of India's best known political families descending from the nation's first prime minister Image: Vipin/AP Photo/picture alliance
Both were released sometime after 8:00 p.m local time (1430 GMT).&#160;
Gandhi and his fellow demonstrators have been demanding meetings with the Election Commission of India, Indian President Droupadi Murmu and a resignation from CEC Kumar.
They claim that Kumar is an agent of the Modi government and has been helping the Bharatiya Janata Party by rigging elections in their favor. These are significant and unprecedented accusations against the ECI&#160;— long regarded as one of the India's&#160;most trusted independent institutions.&#160;&#160;
"I am thankful to every INDIA alliance MP standing firm in defense of our democracy - in Parliament, outside on the streets, and today near Jantar Mantar. They can stop a march. They can refuse a meeting. But they cannot stop the will of our people," Gandhi said on a post on X.&#160; "Narendra Modi, Amit Shah, Gyanesh Kumar and the entire system will be brought to justice," he wrote.
Modi's party and the election body&#160;reject the allegations.
Skip next section Welcome to our coverage! 10/08/2026 October 8, 2026 Welcome to our coverage!
Good morning! And welcome to DW's India blog.&#160;
This is Mahima Kapoor reporting from Studio Delhi, and I will be your guide through the chaos of the Indian news cycle.&#160;
Protests against India's Chief Election Commissioner (CEC), Gyanesh Kumar, over allegations of misconduct and bias, remain at the forefront with opposition leaders planning to stage protests in central Delhi for the third straight day . That's after opposition leader (and Prime Minister Narendra Modi's key competitor) Rahul Gandhi was detained by the police for a second time on Wednesday evening.
CEC Kumar is under fire&#160;for&#160;a controversial revision of electoral rolls in India which have reportedly deleted over 130 million&#160;voters from the official lists since the exercise began in 2025.
Aside from the opposition, the satirical&#160;Cockroach&#160;Janta Party said it will also peacefully demonstrate in the southern city of Bengaluru as it looks to mobilize all parts of the nation to "reclaim our democracy."
On the international front, India's Foreign Ministry said it has sought an update from Russian authorities&#160;on the whereabouts of some 69 Indian nationals. They are part of the group which was&#160;reportedly recruited for the Russian Army.
